  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nAmplifying ridge over the western CONUS with broad surface \nanticyclone over the central CONUS continues to drive mild and dry \nweather conditions across the area. There is a short-wave trough \ndriving a deeper cyclone that is centered over the Canadian Prairie \nProvinces with surface troughing extending southward into the Front \nRange. Over the next couple of days, this will force the center of \nthe surface anticyclone eastward toward the Great Lakes Region, and \nwill likely see some surface pressure falls start to advance into \nthe Central Plains. This will will push a H85 thermal ridge axis \neastward, providing southerly flow into the lower Missouri River \nValley and gradually pushes temperatures back into the upper 70s and \nlower 80s. By the middle of the week the surface cyclone gets lifted \nnorthward back into Canada, which is likely to limit surface \npressure falls into the eastern Plains and lower Missouri River \nValley. Another round dAVA with ridge axis west of the area builds \nanother strong anticyclone across the area, with mid-level flow \ngenerally west-northwesterly. This is lining up to provide downslope \nadiabatic compression into the High Plains, and strengthening WAA as \nwe get into Wednesday and Thursday. If skies continue to remain \nmostly clear, stronger mixing if favored to help temperatures back \ninto the mid 80s across much of the area, with upper 70s in zones \nclose to the Iowa state line. \n\nLater Thursday into early Friday, H5 ridge over the western CONUS \nwill start to break down with the potential for a mid-level vort \nmaxima to traverse the central CONUS. Current deterministic guidance \ndevelops a surface cyclone east of the Rockies and pushes the \nanticyclone toward the Ohio River Valley. This could provide \nconvergence for areas north of Interstate 70 during this time, and \nperhaps could generate light rain activity. The main question will \nbe how much moisture actually returns to the area in the day \npreceding, whether flow brings in moisture from the Gulf, or if it \nis more southwesterly and brings in lower dewpoint air. Ensemble \nsuites continue bounce between solutions brining in rain, and stay \ndry. The current forecast updates places slight chance (15-20 \npercent POPs) into the forecast late Thursday into Friday. For the \nweekend, trough deepens and helps drive more southerly flow into the \narea, which likely keeps temperatures above normal as we progress \nfurther into Fall. However, cloud cover may limit temperatures if \nthe system is able to access more moisture and transport it into the \nregion.",
